<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px 0; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 28px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1 of 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Family and I</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's My Family!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Show comprehension of basic expressions for greeting and leave-taking if supported by gestures. Interactive: Answer simple questions about oneself, using gestures or formulaic expressions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can listen for and understand words related to greetings, leave-takings, and family members (e.g., "hello," "goodbye," "mom," "dad"). Can follow instructions about family-related activities (e.g., "Find a picture of your family."). Can respond to questions about family members, using gestures or single words (e.g., "Who is he?" "Dad").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with "Hello, class!" and models a wave. Students respond with "Hello, teacher!" and wave. The teacher plays a simple "Hello Song" that incorporates waving and pointing to oneself, encouraging students to mimic actions (TPR). Then, the teacher shows large, colorful flashcards of 'mom', 'dad', 'brother', and 'sister', asking students to point to pictures if they hear the word. This activates prior knowledge of greetings and introduces initial family vocabulary visually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a 'mom', clearly saying "Mom. This is my mom." The teacher emphasizes the /m/ sound in "mom". The teacher then holds up a flashcard of a 'dad', clearly saying "Dad. This is my dad." The teacher emphasizes the /d/ sound in "dad". The teacher repeats this with "brother" (/b/ sound) and "sister" (/s/ sound), always using gestures to indicate "my". The teacher uses a slow, clear pace and exaggerated mouth movements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up the 'mom' flashcard) "Is this a dad or a mom?" (Students point or say "Mom")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up the 'dad' flashcard) "Show me dad." (Students point to the 'dad' flashcard or mimic the action for 'dad' if previously modeled)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Family and I")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio story or teacher-read narration about "My Family" using simple sentences and incorporating flashcards. The genre is a descriptive narrative, informal register, presented as an audio input with visual support.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Show comprehension of basic expressions for greeting and leave-taking if supported by gestures. Pragmatic: Introducing family members: Use simple sentences to describe family (e.g., "She is my mom."). Sociolinguistic: Describing Family Roles: Using appropriate language to describe family members and their roles (e.g., "This is my mother.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays a short, simple audio story (or reads it slowly with expression) about a child introducing their family members. The story includes sentences like: "Hello! My name is Lily. This is my mom. She is friendly. This is my dad. He is tall. This is my brother. He likes to play. This is my sister. She loves to smile."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">During the audio, the teacher holds up the corresponding flashcards for 'mom', 'dad', 'brother', 'sister' as each is mentioned, ensuring visual support.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Gist):</b> After listening, the teacher asks general questions: "Who is in Lily's family?" (Students may point to flashcards, or say single words like "Mom, Dad"). "Did Lily say 'hello' or 'goodbye'?" (Students gesture or say 'hello').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Information Matching):</b> The teacher plays the audio again. Students listen and point to the correct flashcard when they hear "mom," "dad," "brother," or "sister." For example, "Listen for 'mom'. Point to mom."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Receptive recognition of "She is," "He is," "my mom," "my dad," "Who is she/he?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> mom, dad, brother, sister, family.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Differentiating /m/, /d/, /b/, /s/ sounds.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listen and Touch):</b> The teacher places flashcards of 'mom', 'dad', 'brother', 'sister' on the board or floor. The teacher says a word (e.g., "mom") and students listen and point or touch the correct flashcard. This is repeated for all four family members, varying the order. The teacher can also say "This is my mom" and students point to 'mom'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Listen and Respond with Gesture/Single Word):</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard and asks, "Who is she?" or "Who is he?" Students listen and respond by pointing to the correct flashcard again, or by saying the single word "Mom" or "Dad." The teacher models the "Who is she?" question multiple times. Choral repetition of "Mom," "Dad."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Listen and Match):</b> Students work in pairs, each with a set of four small flashcards (mom, dad, brother, sister). One student says a family member's name (e.g., "Dad"). The other student listens and finds/holds up the corresponding flashcard. They swap roles after a few turns. The teacher monitors closely, ensuring students are listening accurately and providing help with pronunciation if needed.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Family and I")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Listen and Find" activity using large pictures of families. The focus is on demonstrating listening comprehension in a playful, interactive way. Students will primarily use gestures and pointing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher places large pictures of different families (with distinct 'mom', 'dad', 'brother', 'sister' figures) around the classroom.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives instructions, such as: "Listen! Find a family with a mom." Students walk to and point at a picture containing a mom.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Next, the teacher might say: "Point to the dad." (Students listen and point to the dad in any of the pictures).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher can add more complex instructions as students gain confidence, for example: "Find a sister who is smiling." or "Who is this? (pointing to a specific family member in a picture). Listen and say 'Mom' or 'Dad'."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to listen carefully and respond quickly with gestures or single words. The activity promotes active listening and association of spoken words with visual representations of family members.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will listen to the teacher say a family member's name and point to the correct picture on a worksheet or flashcard set.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can accurately point to 'mom' when heard.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can accurately point to 'dad' when heard.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can accurately point to 'brother' when heard.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can accurately point to 'sister' when heard.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to "Who is he/she?" with a gesture or single word (e.g., "Mom").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I understand 'mom' and 'dad' today?" (Students show thumbs up or thumbs down).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I point to the brother?" (Students point to their own brother if they have one, or a flashcard).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did I hear today?" (Students may point to flashcards or gesture).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to listen and find the family members?" (Students show thumbs up/down).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The activity "Listen and Find" worked well for encouraging active listening and physical response. The students responded positively to the use of flashcards and TPR. Some students found it challenging to differentiate between 'brother' and 'sister' initially, so more specific pronunciation practice and visual cues for these words will be needed in the next lessons. Adjustments for the next lesson will include more repetition of 'brother' and 'sister' sounds and potentially a visual aid that highlights gender. Scaffolds used (gestures, repetition, flashcards) were effective for this grade level and CEFR target.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core family vocabulary through listening. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will use these same vocabulary words in short, illustrated texts, building on the students' receptive knowledge to develop reading comprehension skills related to "My Family and I".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>